## 3. Train/Test Split

### The Concept
- **80% → Training set** — model learns from this
- **20% → Testing set** — model is evaluated on this
- Model **NEVER sees test data during training**
- Testing on same data you trained on = overfitting = meaningless results

### The Exam Analogy
```
Study phase  → model sees X_train + y_train (questions + answers)
Exam phase   → model sees only X_test (new questions, no answers)
Checking     → we compare y_pred vs y_test (did it get them right?)
```

### The 4 Variables Explained

| Variable | What it is | Analogy |
|---|---|---|
| `X_train` | Features of training data | Question papers to study from |
| `y_train` | Labels of training data | Answer key for study questions |
| `X_test` | Features of test data | Exam paper (unseen questions) |
| `y_test` | Labels of test data | Real answers — hidden from model, only WE use these |

### Code
```python
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split

X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(
    X, y,
    test_size=0.2,      # 20% for testing
    random_state=42     # same shuffle every time
)

print("Training samples:", len(X_train))
print("Testing samples:", len(X_test))
```

> **`random_state=42`** — shuffles data the same way every run for reproducibility. Any number works, 42 is just convention.

